<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # A Vessel Is Crossing! – Lesson # 4</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting the Panama Canal</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> A Vessel Is Crossing!</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; " colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Create instructional posters for peers.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can develop short drafts for projects about the canal by describing facts and current activities (e.g., 'The Panama Canal connects two oceans. Vessels are crossing it every day.').
      Can create simple instructional posters on the Panama Canal for peers (e.g., a colorful illustration of the Panama Canal describing vessels passing through with simple sentences: 'The black vessel is bigger than the white vessel.').
      Can edit drafts of project descriptions for clarity (e.g., add another 'n' to the verb 'connects').</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
    <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
    <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of the Panama Canal with different types of vessels (ship, boat, yacht) crossing. Students identify what they see by calling out words (e.g., "boat," "canal," "water"). The teacher asks questions like, "What is happening in this picture?" or "What do you remember about the Panama Canal?" to activate prior knowledge about facts and current activities. This prepares them for writing descriptions.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes simple sentences on the board describing the pictures, modeling target grammar (present simple, present continuous, comparatives) and vocabulary (canal, vessel, ship, connect, travel, big, small, across, through). For example, pointing to a picture: "The Panama Canal <b>connects</b> two <b>oceans</b>." (Present Simple, describing facts) "A <b>vessel is crossing</b> the <b>canal</b> now." (Present Continuous, describing current events) "This <b>ship is bigger than</b> that <b>boat</b>." (Comparative, comparing things). The teacher explicitly spells out key words like 'canal' and 'vessel' to reinforce phonemic awareness related to spelling.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the Panama Canal connect three oceans or two oceans?" (Checks understanding of 'connects' and 'oceans').</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the ship moving now, or was it moving yesterday?" (Checks understanding of Present Continuous 'is crossing' and 'current events').</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal"</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher displays a short, illustrated example of an instructional poster or a series of illustrated captions about the Panama Canal. These examples use simple sentences and familiar vocabulary. For instance, an image of Miraflores Locks with the caption: "Vessels <b>travel through</b> the <b>locks</b>." Another image showing two vessels: "The blue <b>vessel is deeper than</b> the red <b>boat</b>." These serve as models for the writing task.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing facts about the Panama Canal (e.g., 'The Panama Canal connects two oceans.'), describing current events (e.g., 'Vessels are crossing the Canal now.'), and comparing things (e.g., 'That ship is bigger than this yacht.').</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match sentence fragments from the model posters (e.g., "The Panama Canal connects..." with "...two oceans.").</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify "fact sentences" and "activity sentences" from the models. For example, "Is 'The Panama Canal connects oceans' a fact or an activity happening now?"</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Writing</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing descriptive sentences using Present Simple (facts), Present Continuous (current activities), and Comparatives (comparing features) related to the Panama Canal vocabulary (canal, vessel, ship, boat, ocean, connect, travel, big, small, wide, deep, across, through).</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Scramble & Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides sentence strips with jumbled words related to canal facts or activities (e.g., "oceans / two / connects / canal / the / Panama"). Students work individually to reorder the words to form correct sentences. Then, students complete sentences with missing target vocabulary or verb forms (e.g., "A _____ is _____ the canal now." "The Atlantic _____ is _____ than the Pacific _____."). This reinforces spelling and word order.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Guided Picture Description (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> The teacher shows various new pictures of the Panama Canal (e.g., different vessels, the locks, the bridge). For each picture, students are given a sentence starter or a word bank. In pairs, they write 1-2 sentences describing what they see, using present simple, present continuous, or comparatives. Example: "The yellow boat is..." (Students complete: "...smaller than the big ship.") or "People are..." (Students complete: "...watching vessels go through the locks."). They are encouraged to use words like 'across' and 'through'.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Sentence Check:</b> Students exchange their written sentences from activity 2 with another pair. They check each other's sentences for correct spelling (e.g., 'canal,' 'vessel'), grammar, and clarity. The teacher circulates, offering support and highlighting good examples of correct writing, paying attention to pronunciation sounds /k/ and /b/ as students might verbalize their corrections.</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal"</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: Creating Instructional Posters for Peers</b></p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (2-3 students) to create an instructional poster about "Visiting the Panama Canal: A Vessel Is Crossing!". Each group receives a large sheet of paper, markers, and printouts of various images related to the Panama Canal (different vessels, locks, bridge, oceans, people observing). They also receive a checklist of required elements: at least two facts about the canal, two descriptions of current activities, and one comparison between vessels or canal features.</p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group selects images and glues them onto their poster.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They then collaboratively write simple, clear sentences to caption their images, incorporating the target grammar (Present Simple, Present Continuous, Comparatives) and vocabulary (canal, vessel, ship, ocean, connect, travel, big, small, wide, deep, across, through).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For example, one section might have a picture of the canal and say: "The Panama Canal <b>connects</b> the Atlantic and Pacific <b>Oceans</b>." (Describing facts with discourse marker 'It connects...') Another section could show a vessel in the locks: "This <b>vessel is travelling through</b> the <b>locks</b> now." (Describing current events). A third might show two boats: "The red <b>boat is smaller than</b> the white <b>ship</b>." (Comparing things).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to write clearly and neatly for their peers, embodying respectful communication in their written work. The teacher monitors, providing guidance on sentence structure, vocabulary choice, and spelling, ensuring they are blending sounds correctly to form words like 'boat' and 'canal'.</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students display their instructional posters around the classroom. Each group briefly presents their poster to the class, pointing to their written descriptions and reading them aloud. Alternatively, students can do a "gallery walk," observing and reading other groups' posters.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist to assess students' written work on the posters. The checklist focuses on:</p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., canal, vessel, ship, locks, ocean).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and legibility of written sentences.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of Present Simple (for facts).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of Present Continuous (for current activities).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of Comparatives (e.g., bigger than, smaller than).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of required poster elements (facts, activities, comparison).</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write about one fact of the Panama Canal. It is: _______."</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write about one thing a vessel is doing now. It is: _______."</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write about the Panama Canal?"</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult to write? Why?"</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The students were able to create simple instructional posters using the target vocabulary and grammar. The visual aids in the warm-up and presentation stages were effective in jogging their memory and providing models for writing. The guided practice activities helped them construct sentences accurately. Some students struggled with consistently applying the correct verb tense (present simple vs. continuous) or ensuring subject-verb agreement. Spelling of longer words like 'vessel' and 'connects' also required some peer and teacher correction.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">For the next lesson, I will provide more explicit sentence frames for the specific grammar points (e.g., "A ___ is ___ing" for present continuous, or "___ is ___er than ___" for comparatives). I will also emphasize peer editing for spelling and grammar to further consolidate their writing skills.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The posters created in this lesson will serve as a foundational piece for Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), where students will consolidate information and potentially present their findings or use their posters as a reference for a larger class project or guided discussion about the Panama Canal.</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
  </tr>
</table>